1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,660
I was driving alone.

2
00:00:02,510 --> 00:00:04,230
Nobody in the passenger seat.

3
00:00:04,490 --> 00:00:05,930
Nobody in the sleeper.

4
00:00:06,470 --> 00:00:08,690
But for five hundred and forty miles,

5
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:12,320
my truck weighed one hundred
and forty-one pounds too much.

6
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,630
Three scales in a row.

7
00:00:15,020 --> 00:00:18,110
And every one of those pounds
was sitting on the front axle.

8
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:20,620
Right under the passenger seat.

9
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:24,100
Then the sun came up, and
I pulled down the visor.

10
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,200
An old scale ticket fell into my lap.

11
00:00:27,830 --> 00:00:30,200
Somebody had written two
weights on it in pencil.

12
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:34,050
The difference was one
hundred and forty-one pounds.

13
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:37,300
And next to the bigger
number, a woman's name…

14
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:38,530
Hey.

15
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:40,000
It's Hank.

16
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:43,980
Tonight I'm parked at a truck
stop outside Amarillo, Texas.

17
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:48,110
The wind out here is blowing
like it's late for something.

18
00:00:49,020 --> 00:00:52,280
And the coffee tastes like the
pot has been on since Tuesday.

19
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:53,820
I'm drinking it anyway.

20
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:56,550
Tonight's story is about a passenger.

21
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:02,190
A passenger who rode with me across
three states, and I never saw her once.

22
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:05,710
The only one who knew she
was there was the scale.

23
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:09,350
It was Thursday, September
thirteenth, twenty twelve.

24
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:11,870
I was thirty-five years old.

25
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:14,630
My own truck was in the shop that week.

26
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:18,600
Bad injector, and the
part was on back order.

27
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:22,330
So the company gave me a loaner
out of the back row of the yard.

28
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:24,670
Number four oh nine.

29
00:01:25,150 --> 00:01:29,260
An older long-nose truck with a big
sleeper and a lot of miles on it.

30
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:31,210
It was clean inside.

31
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:33,260
Almost too clean.

32
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:36,060
Like nobody had really
lived in it for a while.

33
00:01:36,690 --> 00:01:40,780
I picked up a load of newsprint at a
paper mill in Longview, Washington.

34
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:45,260
Big rolls of paper, the kind
they print newspapers on.

35
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,670
One roll weighs more than a small car.

36
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:52,040
I was taking them to a printing
plant in Salt Lake City.

37
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:54,580
Interstate Five south to Portland.

38
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:58,910
Then Interstate Eighty-Four
east, up the Columbia River,

39
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:02,670
across Oregon, across
Idaho, and down into Utah.

40
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:05,620
About eight hundred and twenty miles.

41
00:02:05,790 --> 00:02:07,130
Two easy days.

42
00:02:07,630 --> 00:02:09,060
At least that's what I thought.

43
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:13,740
Now, if you've never driven a truck,
let me tell you something about weight.

44
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:17,510
A loaded truck is allowed to
weigh eighty thousand pounds.

45
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:20,890
But the total isn't the
only thing that matters.

46
00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:24,740
That weight has to be spread
out right, across the axles.

47
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:26,580
You've got three groups.

48
00:02:27,330 --> 00:02:30,700
The steer axle up front,
under the engine and the cab.

49
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:33,950
The drive axles under
the back of the truck.

50
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:36,660
And the trailer axles, way at the back.

51
00:02:37,270 --> 00:02:38,760
Every group has a limit.

52
00:02:39,670 --> 00:02:41,900
And the steer axle is the touchy one.

53
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:45,830
Put too much weight up front, and
the truck gets heavy in your hands.

54
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:47,660
Harder to turn.

55
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:49,320
Harder on the front tires.

56
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:50,710
Our company had a rule.

57
00:02:51,590 --> 00:02:54,320
Keep the steer axle under
twelve thousand pounds.

58
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:55,740
No exceptions.

59
00:02:56,250 --> 00:03:00,350
So after you load, you go to a
truck stop with a certified scale.

60
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:05,840
You pull onto a long steel platform,
one axle group on each section.

61
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:09,230
You push a button and
talk to somebody inside.

62
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:13,220
A minute later, they hand you a
ticket with three numbers on it.

63
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:15,740
Here's the part most people don't know.

64
00:03:16,570 --> 00:03:18,810
The driver shows up on that ticket too.

65
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:21,070
You sit right over the steer axle.

66
00:03:21,890 --> 00:03:23,170
So does your passenger.

67
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:27,570
If your wife rides along with you,
she's in that steer axle number.

68
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:31,310
Some drivers used to tease
their wives about it.

69
00:03:31,830 --> 00:03:32,990
And one more thing.

70
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:36,500
Diesel weighs about seven pounds a gallon.

71
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:41,170
Every mile you drive, you burn some,
and the truck gets a little lighter.

72
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:43,560
I keep a fuel log.

73
00:03:43,870 --> 00:03:44,890
I always have.

74
00:03:45,330 --> 00:03:48,220
So I always know about how
much fuel I've burned,

75
00:03:48,230 --> 00:03:50,240
and about how much lighter I should be.

76
00:03:50,790 --> 00:03:51,850
Remember that.

77
00:03:52,570 --> 00:03:54,860
A truck only gets lighter as it goes.

78
00:03:55,030 --> 00:03:55,940
Never heavier.

79
00:03:56,490 --> 00:03:59,810
I weighed at a truck stop right
across the road from the mill.

80
00:04:00,710 --> 00:04:01,790
It was about noon.

81
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:05,830
Steer axle, eleven thousand
eight hundred and twenty pounds.

82
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:08,450
Under the line, with room to spare.

83
00:04:08,610 --> 00:04:09,080
Good.

84
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:12,450
I parked at the edge of the
lot to do my paperwork.

85
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,570
Across the street there was an old motel.

86
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:21,330
Two stories, doors facing the parking
lot, and a sign that said weekly rates.

87
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:24,180
I was writing in my
logbook when I heard it.

88
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:26,020
A long, soft hiss.

89
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:27,950
Right next to me.

90
00:04:28,490 --> 00:04:30,550
Trucks like that have air-ride seats.

91
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:35,690
There's a little air bag under the cushion,
and it holds you up like a pillow.

92
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:39,870
When somebody sits down, the
air pushes out with a sigh.

93
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:41,470
I looked over.

94
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:45,310
The passenger seat had sunk
all the way to the bottom.

95
00:04:46,230 --> 00:04:48,130
Like somebody had just sat down in it.

96
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:50,500
There was nobody there, of course.

97
00:04:51,250 --> 00:04:53,180
I figured the air bag had a leak.

98
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:54,900
Old truck, old seat.

99
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:56,220
I pushed on the cushion.

100
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:59,820
It was low and soft,
and it stayed that way.

101
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:01,780
I made a note to tell the shop.

102
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:04,420
And I pulled out onto the interstate.

103
00:05:04,970 --> 00:05:08,030
By the time I got into
the Columbia River Gorge,

104
00:05:08,250 --> 00:05:10,330
I could feel that something was off.

105
00:05:11,130 --> 00:05:12,440
The steering was heavy.

106
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:13,450
Not a lot.

107
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:14,420
Just enough.

108
00:05:14,690 --> 00:05:17,160
Like the truck was leaning
on my arms a little.

109
00:05:17,670 --> 00:05:19,400
I've driven a lot of trucks.

110
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:22,940
You learn how they feel,
the way you learn a horse.

111
00:05:23,710 --> 00:05:25,640
And this one felt loaded up front.

112
00:05:26,150 --> 00:05:28,450
There's a truck stop in
the Gorge with a scale.

113
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:30,870
I pulled in and weighed again.

114
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:33,550
Paid my few dollars and took my ticket.

115
00:05:34,070 --> 00:05:37,600
Steer axle, eleven thousand
nine hundred and forty-one.

116
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:40,210
I sat there and did the math twice.

117
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,020
I'd burned close to twenty-five
gallons since Longview.

118
00:05:44,970 --> 00:05:47,950
By my fuel log, that
steer axle should have

119
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:49,970
been about eleven thousand eight hundred.

120
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:54,020
It was one hundred and
forty-one pounds over that.

121
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:56,050
Nothing else had changed.

122
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:58,340
The drive axles were right.

123
00:05:58,630 --> 00:05:59,790
The trailer was right.

124
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:02,620
All of the extra weight was in one place.

125
00:06:03,430 --> 00:06:04,900
Up front, under the cab.

126
00:06:05,430 --> 00:06:07,480
I went through that
truck with a flashlight.

127
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:08,900
The sleeper.

128
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:09,630
The cabinets.

129
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:10,460
Under the bunk.

130
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:12,720
The side boxes outside.

131
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:13,890
The battery box.

132
00:06:14,630 --> 00:06:16,350
The catwalk behind the cab.

133
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:19,850
I even lay down on the gravel
and looked up under the bumper.

134
00:06:20,390 --> 00:06:21,150
Nothing.

135
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:25,200
Not a toolbox, not a jug
of water, not a stray cat.

136
00:06:25,750 --> 00:06:28,650
And the passenger seat was
still sitting on the bottom.

137
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:30,220
I drove on.

138
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,970
Up out of the Gorge, into the
dry country of eastern Oregon.

139
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:38,920
The wheat stubble there goes
gold all the way to the sky.

140
00:06:39,830 --> 00:06:41,620
I kept both hands on the wheel.

141
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:43,470
The truck kept leaning on me.

142
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:46,370
Near the Idaho line it was getting dark.

143
00:06:47,330 --> 00:06:49,500
I stopped for the night
at a big truck stop.

144
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:52,570
And before I parked, I
weighed one more time.

145
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:56,350
Steer axle, one hundred and
forty-one pounds heavy.

146
00:06:57,250 --> 00:06:57,830
Again.

147
00:06:57,870 --> 00:06:59,870
After four hundred miles of fuel.

148
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:01,480
The same number.

149
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:03,210
To the pound.

150
00:07:03,750 --> 00:07:05,300
Now, a scale can be off.

151
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:07,000
One scale.

152
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:08,100
Maybe two.

153
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:11,120
But three different scales
in three different towns

154
00:07:11,130 --> 00:07:13,830
don't make the same mistake
to the exact pound.

155
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:16,440
So what was riding up front with me?

156
00:07:17,310 --> 00:07:20,880
And why did it weigh exactly one
hundred and forty-one pounds?

157
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:22,950
I didn't sleep much that night.

158
00:07:23,530 --> 00:07:27,910
I lay in the bunk and looked at the passenger
seat through the gap in the curtain.

159
00:07:28,810 --> 00:07:31,450
In the dark, it looked like
somebody was sitting there.

160
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:33,850
Looking out at the parking lot.

161
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:39,100
You know, in most trucks, the passenger
seat is the loneliest seat in America.

162
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,490
It's where you throw your jacket.

163
00:07:41,710 --> 00:07:42,650
Your lunch bag.

164
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:43,560
Your paperwork.

165
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:46,660
Hardly anybody ever sits there.

166
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:49,660
I've had a wife waiting
at home my whole career.

167
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:53,200
And I've spent most of it
talking to an empty seat.

168
00:07:53,530 --> 00:07:54,480
About the weather.

169
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:56,340
About the traffic.

170
00:07:56,670 --> 00:07:57,680
About my day.

171
00:07:57,970 --> 00:08:01,650
You do that long enough, and you
stop noticing that nobody answers.

172
00:08:02,170 --> 00:08:04,390
That night, for the first time in years,

173
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:07,040
I had the feeling that
somebody was listening.

174
00:08:07,630 --> 00:08:09,570
I left at first light, heading east.

175
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:13,570
The sun came up over the
hills and right into my eyes.

176
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:16,420
So I reached up and pulled down the visor.

177
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:20,570
Something slid out from behind
it and dropped into my lap.

178
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:22,530
It was a scale ticket.

179
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:26,620
Old and soft, the ink
faded almost to nothing.

180
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:30,570
The date on it said April
twenty-second, two thousand nine.

181
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:32,600
Same truck number.

182
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:33,630
Four oh nine.

183
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:36,460
Somebody had written
on the back in pencil.

184
00:08:37,350 --> 00:08:39,310
I pulled onto the shoulder to read it.

185
00:08:39,830 --> 00:08:41,900
The first line said, "With June.

186
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:45,210
Eleven thousand four hundred and one."

187
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:48,080
The second line said, "Without June.

188
00:08:48,980 --> 00:08:51,290
Eleven thousand two hundred and sixty."

189
00:08:51,830 --> 00:08:54,860
And under that it said, "She
says the scale is lying.

190
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:56,810
Love you anyway.

191
00:08:56,820 --> 00:08:57,280
D."

192
00:08:57,830 --> 00:08:59,770
I did the math right
there on the shoulder,

193
00:09:00,070 --> 00:09:02,760
with trucks blowing past
and rocking my cab.

194
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:05,560
One hundred and forty-one pounds.

195
00:09:06,050 --> 00:09:07,580
I sat there a long time.

196
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:10,480
Then I looked over at the passenger seat.

197
00:09:10,750 --> 00:09:11,930
Still on the bottom.

198
00:09:12,460 --> 00:09:14,190
"Good morning, June," I said.

199
00:09:15,010 --> 00:09:15,950
I don't know why.

200
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:17,670
It just felt rude not to.

201
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:20,120
At seven o'clock I called the shop.

202
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:22,200
The foreman picked up.

203
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:25,060
A man named Ruben, who had been with that

204
00:09:25,070 --> 00:09:27,530
company longer than the
paint on the building.

205
00:09:28,090 --> 00:09:30,530
I asked him who used
to drive four oh nine.

206
00:09:31,140 --> 00:09:32,680
He was quiet for a second.

207
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:34,620
"Dale Harmon," he said.

208
00:09:35,450 --> 00:09:36,520
"Dale and June.

209
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:38,950
They ran that truck together for years.

210
00:09:39,010 --> 00:09:39,960
She didn't drive.

211
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:42,000
She rode with him.

212
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:45,010
Did the maps, did the
crosswords, kept him awake."

213
00:09:45,550 --> 00:09:46,910
Then he told me the rest.

214
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:51,300
In the spring of twenty ten, they
were waiting on a load in Longview.

215
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:55,380
June wasn't feeling good,
so Dale got her a room

216
00:09:55,390 --> 00:09:57,640
at a little motel across
from the truck stop.

217
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:01,270
She lay down to rest while
he went to get loaded.

218
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:03,680
Her heart stopped while he was gone.

219
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:05,730
She was sixty-one.

220
00:10:06,250 --> 00:10:08,820
"They were two weeks from
retiring," Ruben said.

221
00:10:09,650 --> 00:10:12,090
"Dale drove that truck back here empty.

222
00:10:12,490 --> 00:10:16,500
He parked it in the back row, gave
me the keys, and never drove again."

223
00:10:17,030 --> 00:10:17,850
"Where did he go?"

224
00:10:17,860 --> 00:10:18,630
I asked.

225
00:10:19,150 --> 00:10:20,760
"Home to Idaho," he said.

226
00:10:21,580 --> 00:10:23,480
"Little place called Juniper Flat.

227
00:10:23,730 --> 00:10:24,790
East of Boise."

228
00:10:25,400 --> 00:10:26,940
I asked him one more thing.

229
00:10:27,810 --> 00:10:31,090
I asked if four oh nine had been
back to Longview since then.

230
00:10:31,710 --> 00:10:32,980
I heard him typing.

231
00:10:33,510 --> 00:10:34,500
"No," he said.

232
00:10:35,270 --> 00:10:37,960
"That's the first load she's
pulled out of there since Dale.

233
00:10:38,270 --> 00:10:38,740
Why?

234
00:10:39,560 --> 00:10:40,980
Something wrong with her?"

235
00:10:41,490 --> 00:10:43,100
I looked at the passenger seat.

236
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:44,590
"No," I said.

237
00:10:45,340 --> 00:10:46,670
"She's riding fine."

238
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:48,820
So let me ask you something.

239
00:10:49,330 --> 00:10:51,970
A truck that sat in the
back row for two years.

240
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:57,270
A woman who died in a motel across the
street from that truck stop in Longview.

241
00:10:58,180 --> 00:11:02,970
The first time that truck ever went back,
the passenger seat sighed and sat down.

242
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:06,550
Three scales in three
towns said the same thing.

243
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:09,120
One hundred and forty-one pounds.

244
00:11:09,910 --> 00:11:14,510
The same number a husband once wrote
on a ticket, just to tease his wife.

245
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:17,140
And now that truck was carrying her east.

246
00:11:17,950 --> 00:11:18,660
Toward Idaho.

247
00:11:18,670 --> 00:11:19,560
Toward home.

248
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:21,050
You tell me.

249
00:11:21,590 --> 00:11:23,650
Who was riding in my passenger seat?

250
00:11:24,250 --> 00:11:26,630
I went through Boise in
the middle of the morning.

251
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:32,750
Then out into the high desert, with
sagebrush and black lava rock on both sides.

252
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:36,290
About sixty miles past
Boise, I saw the sign.

253
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:38,910
Juniper Flat, next exit.

254
00:11:39,430 --> 00:11:43,360
And as I came up on that exit, the
steering went light in my hands.

255
00:11:44,170 --> 00:11:45,180
All at once.

256
00:11:45,370 --> 00:11:47,370
Like somebody had let go of my arm.

257
00:11:47,900 --> 00:11:49,180
I heard a soft hiss.

258
00:11:49,950 --> 00:11:50,820
I looked over.

259
00:11:51,060 --> 00:11:53,270
The passenger seat was rising back up.

260
00:11:53,540 --> 00:11:54,400
All the way up.

261
00:11:55,290 --> 00:11:56,870
Like somebody had just stood.

262
00:11:57,420 --> 00:11:58,540
I took the exit.

263
00:11:59,350 --> 00:12:01,460
I don't think I could have driven past it.

264
00:12:02,020 --> 00:12:07,980
Juniper Flat is a gas station, a church,
a grain elevator, and maybe forty houses.

265
00:12:08,810 --> 00:12:11,320
I asked for Dale Harmon
at the gas station.

266
00:12:11,750 --> 00:12:14,400
The lady behind the counter
pointed up the road.

267
00:12:15,030 --> 00:12:16,340
A small white house.

268
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:19,090
Tomato plants along the fence.

269
00:12:19,350 --> 00:12:22,430
And an old man on the porch,
reading the newspaper.

270
00:12:22,980 --> 00:12:24,510
I told him who I was.

271
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:27,640
I told him I was driving four oh nine.

272
00:12:28,050 --> 00:12:29,600
Then I handed him the ticket.

273
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:31,400
He knew it right away.

274
00:12:32,290 --> 00:12:34,530
He held it in both hands and laughed.

275
00:12:34,770 --> 00:12:36,560
And then he wasn't laughing anymore.

276
00:12:37,140 --> 00:12:39,040
"She hated that ticket," he said.

277
00:12:39,850 --> 00:12:41,310
"Hid it from me three times.

278
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:42,520
I always found it."

279
00:12:43,050 --> 00:12:44,540
I told him everything.

280
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:46,540
The seat in Longview.

281
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:47,640
The three scales.

282
00:12:47,650 --> 00:12:48,310
The exit.

283
00:12:48,830 --> 00:12:51,200
I expected him to think I was crazy.

284
00:12:52,100 --> 00:12:53,820
He didn't even look surprised.

285
00:12:54,390 --> 00:12:56,220
"She always said it," Dale said.

286
00:12:57,040 --> 00:12:59,850
"If I go first, I'll
ride the last load home.

287
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:01,140
And I left her there.

288
00:13:02,060 --> 00:13:05,920
I drove home with that seat empty,
and I left her in Longview."

289
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:07,740
He looked up the hill.

290
00:13:08,460 --> 00:13:10,630
There was a little cemetery
up there with a white

291
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:13,670
fence around it, looking
down at the interstate.

292
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:16,980
"You can see the exit
from her grave," he said.

293
00:13:17,790 --> 00:13:20,230
"I picked the spot so she
could watch the trucks."

294
00:13:20,750 --> 00:13:22,530
We sat on his porch for an hour.

295
00:13:23,380 --> 00:13:24,890
He made me a sandwich.

296
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:27,790
He told me June used to
call herself the ballast.

297
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:30,170
She said that was her job.

298
00:13:30,380 --> 00:13:31,700
To keep him on the road.

299
00:13:32,270 --> 00:13:34,540
When I left, he walked
me out to the truck.

300
00:13:35,370 --> 00:13:39,420
He put his hand flat on the passenger
door and kept it there a while.

301
00:13:40,390 --> 00:13:41,690
He didn't say anything.

302
00:13:41,810 --> 00:13:42,790
He didn't need to.

303
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:47,300
At the next scale, my steer axle was
exactly what it should have been.

304
00:13:48,150 --> 00:13:49,150
To the pound.

305
00:13:49,670 --> 00:13:50,910
There's one more thing.

306
00:13:51,750 --> 00:13:53,940
And I've never been able to explain it.

307
00:13:54,460 --> 00:13:56,530
I brought four oh nine back to the yard.

308
00:13:57,470 --> 00:14:00,980
I told Ruben the passenger
seat needed a new air bag.

309
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:04,480
That it had been sitting on
the bottom since Longview.

310
00:14:05,050 --> 00:14:06,880
He climbed in and looked under it.

311
00:14:07,770 --> 00:14:10,440
Then he climbed back out
and gave me a funny look.

312
00:14:10,970 --> 00:14:11,990
"Hank," he said.

313
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:14,380
"That seat doesn't have any air.

314
00:14:14,700 --> 00:14:17,340
Dale had me cap the line after June died.

315
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:20,510
He didn't want anybody
sitting in her seat."

316
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:23,490
That seat had no air in it for two years.

317
00:14:24,290 --> 00:14:25,900
It couldn't sigh for anybody.

318
00:14:26,420 --> 00:14:27,620
But I heard it sigh.

319
00:14:28,430 --> 00:14:29,300
In Longview.

320
00:14:29,550 --> 00:14:31,320
And I heard it again at that exit.

321
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:33,580
So here's what I'll leave you with.

322
00:14:34,190 --> 00:14:37,550
If somebody has been asking to
ride along with you, take them.

323
00:14:38,470 --> 00:14:40,950
On a run, on a trip,
to the hardware store.

324
00:14:41,240 --> 00:14:42,660
Whatever your road is.

325
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:44,820
Don't wait for retirement.

326
00:14:45,040 --> 00:14:46,990
Dale and June were two weeks short.

327
00:14:47,610 --> 00:14:50,260
The seat next to you won't
always have somebody in it.

328
00:14:51,170 --> 00:14:52,520
Fill it while you can.

329
00:14:53,070 --> 00:14:55,800
Next time, I've got
another one from the road.

330
00:14:56,300 --> 00:14:58,070
And now I want to hear from you.

331
00:14:58,980 --> 00:15:00,470
Who rode in your passenger seat?

332
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:03,070
A wife, a buddy, your dad, a dog?

333
00:15:03,980 --> 00:15:05,780
Tell me about them in the comments.

334
00:15:05,950 --> 00:15:07,130
I read every one.

335
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:09,070
Drive safe out there.

336
00:15:09,590 --> 00:15:14,160
And if your truck ever weighs a little
heavy, don't be so quick to blame the scale.

337
00:15:15,100 --> 00:15:17,410
Maybe somebody just needs a ride home.
